      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. The markers or behaviors that clients often associate with exclusivity and refinement in the context you serve generally include a certain level of personal demeanor, choice of language, speech patterns, non-verbal cues, and a demonstrated understanding of the established norms within their elite professional network. Community-based backgrounds might unfortunately project behaviors—such as more direct communication, communal decision-making, or a lack of industry-specific jargon—that can come across as less formal, less tailored to the unique cultures of high-net-worth client groups, and therefore, a bit more unsophisticated or dissonant.\n\nAdditionally, there could be an absence of an inherent understanding of the implicit social codes and rituals—like knowing when to express personal interest in a high-net-worth client or when to lead negotiations—that are second nature to those already within that privileged circle. It's not necessarily these behaviors that are negative; it's the mismatched perception that they don't exude the same level of 'insider' authenticity as someone whose every experience and action has woven them seamlessly into the fabric of that community. So while Candidate B may have strengths in community ethos and connectivity, they may lack certain subtleties that facilitate smoother, more instinctual interactions with your current clientele, which might explain why they seem an",
